11/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "89\\,850"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d7b17f93243c3b4c733cf6573512f1f095b38177d272887b810f6bb0fb50db4" "basis" 1307 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-316149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(4)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-316149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=4,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(4)={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-316149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-316149" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-316149" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-346555" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346555" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(1)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=1,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(1)={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =5({e}^{2(1)+1}-{e}^{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{3}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-346545" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346545" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e^3-5e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-346530" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346530" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{22}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/edcecf885aaf18a3aa6f6726c0485b1885e37132e73c8964de2576f7da5d61bf.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=0,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/bc8905f82a9f7525d6c59df337138e9f3b0417a45bc40f4b76fee2ddedac2eab.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,-2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,-2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+(6-\\pi )=7-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5-\\pi "] ["math" "6+\\pi "] ["math" "6-\\pi "] ["math" "7+\\pi "] ["math" "7-\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7-\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10+\\pi "] ["math" "10-\\pi "] ["math" "6-\\pi "] ["math" "8+\\pi "] ["math" "8-\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10-\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/e0fc07ac8466381b668877e25cd0c9d8d5258a9061548e7b8efb12f75f6a0f04.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/923b60ed63551bda207f7e3eeb09e62f8b8eb7465a488858bef54bc36ab20baa.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4+2\\pi "] ["math" "5+2\\pi "] ["math" "6+\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{6+\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{8+\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-49220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6+\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18+9\\pi "] ["math" "23+9\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{36+9\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{45+18\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The bacteria culture in a lab increases at a rate of ${e}^{2t}$ per hour. If, after $2$ hours, there are $2\\,000$ bacteria in the population, approximately how many bacteria are in the population after $6$ hours?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ be the rate at which the number of bacteria is changing and let $S(T)$ be the total number of bacteria and in the population after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+{\\int }_{2}^{T}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}{|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of bacteria in the culture after $T=6$ hours, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(6)=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{12}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}≈83\\,350.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "69\\,270"] ["math" "73\\,225"] ["math" "78\\,250"] ["math" "83\\,350"] ["math" "89\\,850"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "83\\,350"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Thermal energy is being added to a device at a rate of $3t$ joules per second. If at time $t=0$ the device already contains $15$ joules of energy, write an expression for $E(t),$ the total energy in the device at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n$E(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t)=3t$ represent the rate at which energy enters the device, and let $E(T)$ be the total energy in the device after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nE(T)=E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(T) & =E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={15+(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+\\frac{3{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nE(t)=15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-346571" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346571" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15+\\frac{3}{2}t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The weight, in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth grows at a rate of $3{t}^{2}+15.$ If, at $t=1$ years old, the gorilla weighed $20$ pounds, how much will the gorilla weigh at $3$ years old?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $s(t)=3{t}^{2}+15$ be the rate at which the weight of the gorilla is changing and let $S(T)$ be the weight of the gorilla $T$ years after birth. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+{\\int }_{1}^{T}3{t}^{2}+15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+({t}^{3}+15t){|}_{1}^{T} \\\\  & =20+({T}^{3}+15T)-({1}^{3}+15\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20+{T}^{3}+15T-16 \\\\  & ={T}^{3}+15T+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the weight of the gorilla $T=3$ years after birth, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(3)={3}^{3}+15\\cdot 3+4=76.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "59"] ["math" "66"] ["math" "72"] ["math" "76"] ["math" "81"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-103777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "76"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A paddling pool contains $6$ gallons of water initially. At $t=0,$ water is pumped into the pool at a constant rate of $3$ gallons per minute. After $t=2$ minutes, water starts leaking from the pool at a rate of $\\sqrt{t-2}$ gallons per minute. How many gallons of water are in the pool at time $t$, for $t\\ge 2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t)=3-\\sqrt{t-2}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing for $t\\ge 2.$ Note that we subtract $\\sqrt{t-2}$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $F(T)$ be the volume of the pool after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T)=F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the pool contains $6+2\\cdot 3=12$ gallons of water. Substituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{2}^{T}(3-\\sqrt{t-2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{(3t-\\frac{2}{3}{(t-2)}^{3/2})|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =12+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}-(3(2)-\\frac{2}{3}(2-2)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =6+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of our original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "F(t)=3t-\\frac{1}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}"] ["math" "F(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}"] ["math" "F(t)=6t+\\frac{1}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}"] ["math" "F(t)=6t+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}"] ["math" "F(t)=9t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{5/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-90779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "F(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In $2010$, a group of biologists started tracking the population of wolves in a mountain reserve. The initial population was $250,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $6+2t$ and $2+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2010.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of wolves in the reserve at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{6+2t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(2+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4+1.5t.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of wolves $T$ years after $2010.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4+1.5t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+(4t+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =250+4T+\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99dd4a43c7f873a7c87243b837f1c147d7a0fa07940e5726459ae997373b39ca"} {"question" "q-346580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "q-346580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "250+4t+\\frac{3}{4}t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/state.json" "file_sha256" "09668b41e87730e49a501a872b72fb197e129fc553b00cede91e8d731e887f56"} {"question" "e-10876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10876.json" "file_sha256" "80d57b28d1dd12653b9b55554ed98c3e1faad441e5e75b8a638b0fb308d9f19d"} {"question" "e-10876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi ) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10876.json" "file_sha256" "80d57b28d1dd12653b9b55554ed98c3e1faad441e5e75b8a638b0fb308d9f19d"} {"question" "e-10884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10884.json" "file_sha256" "a732d10d7adeaa97fe0793f154d523cbd50225ebf95630fba086e19829c1e35c"} {"question" "e-10884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10884.json" "file_sha256" "a732d10d7adeaa97fe0793f154d523cbd50225ebf95630fba086e19829c1e35c"} {"question" "e-10877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a pool has a small hole and that water is leaking out at a rate of $t^{1/3}$ gallons per minute, where $t$ is the time in minutes. If at time $t = 0$ the pool contains $100$ gallons of water, how many gallons of water will remain in the pool after $t$ minutes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10877.json" "file_sha256" "639adaf90c0483255a26bda71870b0a565f2ec797461e44d9f54c7fca0627fe4"} {"question" "e-10877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t) = -t^{1/3}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) \t\t\t= F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-{\\int }_{0}^{T}{t}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3{t}^{4/3}}{4}{|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3(T)^{4/3}}{4}+\\frac{3(0)^{4/3}}{4} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{4/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/example-10877.json" "file_sha256" "639adaf90c0483255a26bda71870b0a565f2ec797461e44d9f54c7fca0627fe4"}] "usage" {"e-10884" [] "q-103776" [] "e-10877" [] "q-103770" [] "q-103771" [] "e-10876" [] "q-156193" []} "basis" 1414 "retractions" [[17592186289451 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$"] [17592186289451 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186289437 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$"] [17592186289437 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299252 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$"] [17592186299252 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063842 :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)"] [17592186063842 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186289439 17592186289440 17592186289441 17592186289442 17592186289443 17592186289453 17592186289454 17592186289455 17592186289456 17592186289457 17592186299254 17592186299255 17592186299256 17592186299257 17592186299258 17592186299261 17592186299262 17592186299263 17592186299264 17592186299265] "immutable_fields" [[17592186289438 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289438 :answer-field/id] [17592186289438 :answer-field/key] [17592186289438 :answer-field/type] [17592186289452 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289452 :answer-field/id] [17592186289452 :answer-field/key] [17592186289452 :answer-field/type] [17592186299253 :answer